Q4 – Thread Creation and the Thread Life Cycle (7 Marks)
This question focuses on Java multithreading. We simulate four examination scanner stations working simultaneously to scan answer scripts.

The assignment requires two implementations:
1. Extending the Thread class.
2. Implementing the Runnable interface.
It also requires testing start(), sleep(), join(), isAlive(), thread priorities and daemon threads.
1. Shared Group Work
A. Method 1 – Extending the Thread class
In this method, we create a class that extends Thread and override its run() method.

class ScannerStation extends Thread {

    int scripts;

    ScannerStation(String name, int scripts) {
        super(name);
        this.scripts = scripts;
    }

    public void run() {
        int count = 0;

        try {
            for (int i = 1; i <= scripts; i++) {
                System.out.println(getName() +
                    " scanned script " + i);

                count++;
                Thread.sleep(200);
            }

            System.out.println(getName() +
                " finished scanning " + count + " scripts");

        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println(getName() + " interrupted");
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
}

public class ThreadExample {

    public static void main(String[] args)
            throws InterruptedException {

        ScannerStation s1 = new ScannerStation("Station 1", 3);
        ScannerStation s2 = new ScannerStation("Station 2", 3);
        ScannerStation s3 = new ScannerStation("Station 3", 3);
        ScannerStation s4 = new ScannerStation("Station 4", 3);

        s1.start();
        s2.start();
        s3.start();
        s4.start();

        System.out.println("Station 1 alive: " + s1.isAlive());

        s1.join();
        s2.join();
        s3.join();
        s4.join();

        System.out.println("All stations completed.");
        System.out.println("Total scripts scanned: " +
            (3 + 3 + 3 + 3));
    }
}



Explanation of methods
Method	Purpose
start()	Starts a new thread and executes its run method.
run()	Contains the task performed by the thread.
sleep(200)	Pauses the current thread for approximately 200 milliseconds.
join()	Makes the calling thread wait until another thread finishes.
isAlive()	Checks whether a thread has started and has not yet terminated.

Expected output (one possible order)
Station 1 scanned script 1
Station 2 scanned script 1
Station 3 scanned script 1
Station 4 scanned script 1
Station 1 scanned script 2
Station 3 scanned script 2
Station 2 scanned script 2
Station 4 scanned script 2
...
Station 1 finished scanning 3 scripts
Station 2 finished scanning 3 scripts
Station 3 finished scanning 3 scripts
Station 4 finished scanning 3 scripts
All stations completed.
Total scripts scanned: 12


The exact order can change on every execution.
B. Method 2 – Implementing the Runnable interface
In this approach, we implement Runnable and pass its object to a Thread object.

class ScannerTask implements Runnable {

    int scripts;
    String stationName;

    ScannerTask(String stationName, int scripts) {
        this.stationName = stationName;
        this.scripts = scripts;
    }

    public void run() {
        try {
            for (int i = 1; i <= scripts; i++) {

                System.out.println(stationName +
                    " scanned script " + i);

                Thread.sleep(200);
            }

            System.out.println(stationName +
                " finished scanning " + scripts + " scripts");

        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println(stationName + " interrupted");
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
}

public class RunnableExample {

    public static void main(String[] args)
            throws InterruptedException {

        Thread s1 = new Thread(new ScannerTask("Station 1", 3));
        Thread s2 = new Thread(new ScannerTask("Station 2", 3));
        Thread s3 = new Thread(new ScannerTask("Station 3", 3));
        Thread s4 = new Thread(new ScannerTask("Station 4", 3));

        s1.start();
        s2.start();
        s3.start();
        s4.start();

        s1.join();
        s2.join();
        s3.join();
        s4.join();

        System.out.println("All stations completed.");
        System.out.println("Total scripts scanned: 12");
    }
}



